    "def": "Why storing comma-separated IDs or numbered columns (tag1, tag2, tag3) breaks indexing, referential integrity, and relational querying. Core Invariant & Mechanics: Why storing comma-separated IDs or numbered columns (tag1, tag2, tag3) breaks indexing, referential integrity, and relational querying. Core Invariant: A relation is in Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) iff for every non-trivial functional dependency X → Y, X is a superkey; preserving dependency closure F+ and lossless-join decomposition (R1 ∩ R2 → R1 or R2) prevents spurious tuples. Storage & Production Reality: Heavy OLTP update paths require 3NF/BCNF so updating an attribute touches a single 8KB heap page; read-heavy paths selectively denormalize with triggers or materialized views when join latency breaches SLA. (First Normal Form (1NF): Atomic Domains & Repeating Group Anti-Patterns)",
