    "term": "Production Case Study: Anatomy of a Transaction ID (XID) Wraparound Outage",
    "def": "Dissecting how unvacuumed 32-bit transaction IDs approach the 2-billion horizon, forcing PostgreSQL into emergency read-only shutdown to prevent data loss. Core Invariant & Mechanics: Dissecting how unvacuumed 32-bit transaction IDs approach the 2-billion horizon, forcing PostgreSQL into emergency read-only shutdown to prevent data loss. Core Invariant: Under Snapshot Isolation (MVCC), readers never block writers and writers never block readers; a tuple version V is visible to snapshot S iff V.xmin is committed before S and V.xmax is either invalid or uncommitted/after S. Storage & Production Reality: Long-running uncommitted transactions pin OldestXmin backward, preventing VACUUM / purge threads from reclaiming dead MVCC tuple versions and causing severe table/index bloat and transaction ID wraparound risk. (Production Case Study: Anatomy of a Transaction ID (XID) Wraparound Outage)",
