    "term": "The Classic Outer Join Trap: Filtering in ON vs Filtering in WHERE",
    "def": "Why placing a predicate on the right-hand table inside WHERE silently converts a LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN by rejecting NULL-extended rows. Core Invariant & Mechanics: Why placing a predicate on the right-hand table inside WHERE silently converts a LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN by rejecting NULL-extended rows. Core Invariant: An INNER JOIN filters unmatched rows, whereas a LEFT OUTER JOIN preserves every left tuple by extending unmatched right attributes with NULL; placing a non-null-tolerant predicate on the right table in WHERE collapses a LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN. Storage & Production Reality: Hash Join builds an in-memory open-addressed hash table over the smaller build relation (falling back to Grace Hash Join disk partitions if work_mem is exceeded), whereas Merge Join streams pre-sorted B+Tree inputs in O(1) memory. (The Classic Outer Join Trap: Filtering in ON vs Filtering in WHERE)",
